{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00521 - MONDO_6875",
  "verbalization": "Carteolol, a beta-blocker, is implicated in the pathogenesis of ocular hypertension through its association with open-angle glaucoma, a condition that is itself linked to ocular hypertension. The evidence suggests that Carteolol may exert a modulatory effect on the ocular microcirculation, potentially influencing intraocular pressure through mechanisms involving vascular tone and fluid dynamics. The shared indication of open-angle glaucoma across multiple pathways implies a possible role in the pathophysiology of ocular hypertension, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated. The presence of similar indications with different drugs (e.g., Befunolol and Levobetaxolol) further supports the idea that beta-blockers may contribute to the development of ocular hypertension through shared pathophysiological pathways. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Carteolol to be considered a candidate for repurposing in the treatment of ocular hypertension, provided further mechanistic validation is obtained.",
